
SQL Performance Optimization (Interview Questions & Real-Time Examples)

SQL Performance Optimization is the process of making SQL queries execute faster by reducing CPU, memory, disk I/O, and execution time.

1. How do you optimize a slow SQL query?
..............................................
Steps
Use EXPLAIN to analyze the query.
Create proper indexes.
Avoid SELECT *.
Retrieve only required rows.
Optimize JOINs.
Replace correlated subqueries with JOINs or CTEs where appropriate.
Partition large tables.
Keep database statistics updated.

2. Use EXPLAIN
..............................................
Before optimization:

EXPLAIN
SELECT *
FROM Customer
WHERE CustomerId = 10;

Output:

type : ALL
rows : 1000000
Extra : Using where

This indicates a Full Table Scan.

3. Create an Index
..............................................
Without Index

SELECT *
FROM Customer
WHERE Email='sunil@gmail.com';

Searches every row.

Create Index

CREATE INDEX idx_email
ON Customer(Email);

Now the database can locate the matching rows much faster.

4. Avoid SELECT *
..............................................
❌ Bad

SELECT *
FROM Customer;

✅ Good

SELECT CustomerId,
       CustomerName
FROM Customer;

Benefits:

Less memory usage
Less network traffic
Better index utilization
5. Use WHERE Clause

❌

SELECT *
FROM Transactions;

✅

SELECT *
FROM Transactions
WHERE TransactionDate='2026-08-01';

Reads fewer rows.

6. Use Proper JOINs
..............................................
❌

SELECT *
FROM Customer c,
     Account a
WHERE c.CustomerId=a.CustomerId;

✅

SELECT c.CustomerName,
       a.Balance
FROM Customer c
INNER JOIN Account a
ON c.CustomerId=a.CustomerId;

7. EXISTS vs IN
..............................................
Large Data

SELECT *
FROM Customer c
WHERE EXISTS
(
    SELECT 1
    FROM Account a
    WHERE a.CustomerId=c.CustomerId
);

Small List

SELECT *
FROM Customer
WHERE CustomerId IN (1,2,3);
8. WHERE vs HAVING

❌

SELECT AccountType,
       COUNT(*)
FROM Account
GROUP BY AccountType
HAVING AccountType='Savings';

✅

SELECT AccountType,
       COUNT(*)
FROM Account
WHERE AccountType='Savings'
GROUP BY AccountType;

WHERE filters before grouping, making it more efficient.

9. Composite Index
CREATE INDEX idx_name_city
ON Customer(CustomerName, Address);

Useful Query

SELECT *
FROM Customer
WHERE CustomerName='Sunil'
AND Address='Nellore';
10. Covering Index
CREATE INDEX idx_customer
ON Customer(CustomerId, CustomerName);

Query

SELECT CustomerId,
       CustomerName
FROM Customer
WHERE CustomerId=100;

The database may satisfy the query using only the index.

11. Avoid Functions on Indexed Columns

❌

SELECT *
FROM Customer
WHERE UPPER(CustomerName)='SUNIL';

May prevent index usage.

✅

SELECT *
FROM Customer
WHERE CustomerName='Sunil';
12. Pagination
SELECT *
FROM Customer
LIMIT 10 OFFSET 20;

Returns rows 21–30 instead of the entire table.

13. Use LIMIT
..............................................
❌

SELECT *
FROM Account
ORDER BY Balance DESC;

✅

SELECT *
FROM Account
ORDER BY Balance DESC
LIMIT 10;
14. Optimize ORDER BY

Create Index

CREATE INDEX idx_balance
ON Account(Balance);

Query

SELECT *
FROM Account
ORDER BY Balance DESC;
15. Avoid Correlated Subqueries When Possible

❌

SELECT CustomerName
FROM Customer c
WHERE EXISTS
(
    SELECT 1
    FROM Account a
    WHERE a.CustomerId=c.CustomerId
);

A correlated query can be efficient in some cases, but if it executes repeatedly over very large datasets, consider rewriting it.

✅ JOIN Version
..............................................
SELECT DISTINCT c.CustomerName
FROM Customer c
JOIN Account a
ON c.CustomerId=a.CustomerId;
16. Partition Large Tables

Example

Transactions_2024

Transactions_2025

Transactions_2026

Instead of searching millions of rows in one table.

17. Delete vs Truncate

Delete

DELETE FROM Transactions;

Deletes row by row.

Truncate

TRUNCATE TABLE Transactions;

Faster when removing all rows.

18. Batch Updates

❌

UPDATE Customer
SET Address='Hyderabad';

Updates every row.

✅

UPDATE Customer
SET Address='Hyderabad'
WHERE CustomerId=101;
19. Index Scan vs Index Seek
Index Seek	Index Scan
Searches only matching rows	Reads many index entries
Very Fast	Slower
Preferred	Used when necessary
20. Full Table Scan

Occurs when no suitable index exists.

Example

SELECT *
FROM Customer
WHERE Email='abc@gmail.com';

Without an index on Email, the database scans every row.

21. Execution Plan
EXPLAIN
SELECT *
FROM Account
WHERE Balance > 50000;

Check:

Access type
Possible keys
Chosen key
Rows examined
Extra information
22. Real-Time Banking Example
Problem

Finding an account by account number takes 10 seconds.

SELECT *
FROM Account
WHERE AccountNo=1001;
Solution
CREATE INDEX idx_account
ON Account(AccountNo);

Now the database can locate the account directly
